<p>from a technical standpoint, this is just all over the place. There's a lot of scientific-*sounding* things, but they don't really fit. It left me with a lot of questions and a couple observations:</p>
<ul>
<li>Why use UV/IR vision to look at it? Can you safely look at it though camera? There are photographs of it, so I assume so.</li>
<li>Is it radio frequency anechoic chamber or acoustic anechoic chamber? The picture appears to be of an RF testing lab. I'd recommend making it an RF chamber, since it seems that's how it detects the auroras</li>
<li>It seems like a simple light sensor could work to detect the increased light output. Why is the Schroedinger equation used?</li>
<li>Why does it emit cold? That sounds like a violation of thermodynamics, though it could just be fridge logic.</li>
<li>Why does it enter the bone marrow, then pull the neurotransmitters out? I don't think neurotransmitters live in bones.</li>
<li>Molten glass takes a long time to safely solidify and is hard to handle; if it's cooled too quickly it will shatter. Instead of glass you could maybe use a quick-hardening resin, but does it need to be a liquid that hardens, or could it just be a solid?</li>
<li>How do radioisotopes help track it? Radioactive tracers are used to understand how chemical reactions happen, not track things in space.</li>
</ul>
<p>While the concept is somewhat interesting, and the images are great, I don't think it fits together very well. -1</p>

